<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Verifying Memoryless Sequential Decision-making
of Large Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dennis Gross
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Helge Spieker
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Arnaud Gotlieb
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a tool for rigorous, automated verification of large language model (LLM)–based policies in memoryless sequential decision-making tasks. Given a Markov decision process (MDP) representing the sequential decision-making task, an LLM policy, and a safety requirement expressed as a PCTL formula, our approach incrementally constructs only the reachable portion of the MDP guided by LLM-chosen actions. Each state is encoded as a natural-language prompt, the LLM’s response is parsed into an action, and reachable successor states by the policy are expanded. The resulting formal model is checked with Storm to determine whether the policy satisfies the specified safety property. In experiments on standard grid-world benchmarks, we show that open-source LLMs accessed via Ollama can be verified—when deterministically seeded—but generally underperform deep reinforcement learning baselines. Our tool natively integrates with Ollama and supports PRISM-specified tasks, enabling continuous benchmarking in user-specified sequential decision-making tasks and laying a practical foundation for formally verifying increasingly capable LLMs.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">keywords</h6>
Large Language Models ,Model Checking ,Ethical and Trustworthy AI

</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span>Simula Research Laboratory, Oslo, Norway</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">large language models (LLMs)</em> have shown remarkable promise in <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sequential decision-making tasks</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarukkai et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In these scenarios, an agent repeatedly observes the current <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">environment state</em>, selects an <em id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">action</em> (potentially invoking external tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hayet et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li (2025)</a></cite>), and transitions—often under uncertainty—to a new state, all while striving to maximize long-term reward.
Throughout this work, we assume the <em id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Markov property</em>, which ensures that the probability of any future state depends solely on the present state and not on the path taken to reach it.
Therefore, it is enough to use <em id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">memoryless policies</em> that map each state directly to an action, without referencing past history <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baier and Katoen (2008)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, consider a grid-world taxi domain, in which the agent’s state comprises its position, fuel level, and passenger status <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gross et al. (2022)</a></cite>. At each step, the taxi must decide whether to move, refuel, or pick up/drop off a passenger based on the current state. A well-designed policy ensures the taxi avoids running out of fuel while completing its pickups and drop-offs efficiently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs generate text by predicting the next token (interpretable as part of a word) based on all preceding tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. (2017)</a></cite>. Framed as a decision agent, an LLM can receive a prompt such as:</p>
<blockquote id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p">“Fuel level is below 10%. Should the taxi go to the gas station or continue driving to the passenger drop-off location?”</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Its autoregressive output yields a chosen action, which then transitions the system to a new state and closes the feedback loop. In this manner, an LLM implements a policy that maps states to actions over time, enabling its use in sequential decision-making domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite their flexibility, LLM-based policies lack formal guarantees, making them prone to unsafe behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2023)</a></cite>—such as depleting fuel or causing collisions—and often less reliable or optimal than classical methods like trained deep reinforcement learning policies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Model checking</em> provides a formal verification framework that constructs a mathematical model of the system and proves whether it satisfies a given specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baier and Katoen (2008)</a></cite>. When properties involve probabilistic outcomes, they can be expressed in <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">probabilistic computation tree logic (PCTL)</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2020)</a></cite>, yielding provable safety guarantees.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">While prior work has begun to take LLM safety into account <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2023)</a></cite>, none has offered rigorous verification of their behavior in sequential decision-making tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce a rigorous model-checking tool for verifying LLM-based policies in memoryless sequential decision-making tasks against complex safety properties. Our approach accepts five inputs:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Markov decision process (MDP)</em> defining the environment dynamics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">LLM-based policy</em> that proposes actions given states.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">PCTL formula</em> specifying the desired safety property.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">input encoder function</em> that maps the state into an LLM input prompt.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">action parser function</em> that parses the action from the LLM output.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">We <em id="S1.p7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">incrementally build</em> only the reachable portion of the MDP under the LLM policy, encoding each state as a natural-language prompt that includes task instructions and an output-format directive. We query the LLM, parse its response into an action, and expand only the resulting successor states.
Once the relevant states and state-transitions are constructed, we leverage the Storm model checker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hensel et al. (2022)</a></cite> to verify compliance of the LLM policy in combination with the input encoder and action parser function in the environment with the PCTL specification.
We control the random seeds to keep the LLM outputs for the same inputs constant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments demonstrate that state-of-the-art open-source LLMs—accessed via the open-source LLM platform Ollama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marcondes et al. (2025)</a></cite> to run LLMs locally—can be subjected to this verification process, provided they yield deterministic outputs for identical prompts. Although these models currently underperform classical baselines (e.g., deep reinforcement learning agents<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gross et al. (2022)</a></cite>), our tool enables seamless verification of new LLM releases and user-defined tasks specified in the PRISM language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">pri (????)</a></cite>, facilitating continuous benchmarking and laying the groundwork for formally certifying future, more capable LLMs in memoryless sequential decision making.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we set our LLM verification paper into context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs are neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky et al. (2012)</a></cite> and built on top of the transformer technology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. (2017)</a></cite>.
Ollama is an open-source, cross-platform tool that enables the downloading and local execution of open-source LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marcondes et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
It provides a REST API for easy interaction with LLMs such as Meta Llama models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Mistral 7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite>, Gemma 3 model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamath et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and others such as DeepSeek models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
We utilize Ollama as the interface to a wide range of state-of-the-art LLMs.
These LLMs have presented increasingly emerging abilities in various tasks, such as sequential decision-making tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarukkai et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of sequential decision-making evaluation, Qin et al. evaluate the capabilities of LLMs using metrics based on Monte Carlo methods for dynamic evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
They set up random, reinforcement learning, and LLM players for comparison.
Wang et al. use the Avalon, which contains elements of deception, to evaluate the capability of LLMs to recognize and handle deceptive information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Gong et al. leverage the CuisineWorld and Minecraft to assess the planning
and emergency cooperation capabilities of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
To summarize, various evaluation papers exist for sequential decision-making problems.
However, these papers do not allow for an exact comparison of LLMs, and each paper is tailored to a specific use case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Verification can help, and there is an increasing interest in the safety and trustworthiness of LLMs through the lens of verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tegmark and Omohundro (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Various studies use model checking to verify that synthesized sequential decision-making policies do not exhibit unsafe behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eliyahu et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazak et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Corsi et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dräger et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gross et al. (2022)</a></cite>; however, none verify the sequential decision-making of LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A big challenge of formally verifying neural networks is that most verification methods depend on the neural network architecture and size <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ehlers (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
However, by employing an incremental building process as a model-agnostic approach for the verification of sequential decision-making capabilities of LLMs with respect to safety properties, we remain independent of the underlying LLM architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">COOL‑MC is a tool that combines <em id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reinforcement learning (RL)</em> with formal methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gross et al. (2022)</a></cite>, enabling rigorous training, verification, and interpretability of RL policies.
It incrementally builds a formal model of the policy’s interaction with a sequential decision-making task, represented as an MDP, and verifies safety properties using the Storm model checker.
We extend this incremental building process by integrating LLMs into it by using Ollama as an interface between LLMs and COOL-MC, and handling the translation from numerical states to LLM inputs and LLM outputs to actions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">La Malfa et al. formalize the concept of semantic robustness, which generalizes the notion of natural language processing (NLP) robustness by explicitly considering the robustness measurement of cogent linguistic phenomena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malfa and Kwiatkowska (2022)</a></cite>.
There exists a variety of robustness verification algorithms for transformers in classification tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
Our work differs in that we are interested in verifying the memoryless sequential decision-making capabilities of LLMs with respect to PCTL properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Notable in the context of COOL-MC and LLMs, there exists a paper that tries to explain the black box nature of deep RL policy actions and enhance their performance via an LLM model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gross and Spieker (2024)</a></cite>.
The difference to our work is that we use LLMs as the sequential decision-making policies.
Furthermore, recent work uses LLMs to translate natural language to temporal logic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cosler et al. (2023)</a></cite> and another recent work that combines formal verification with LLMs focuses on detecting hallucinations through logical consistency checks.
They use generated counter-examples to guide the LLM to improve its performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jha et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">While VeriPlan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2025)</a></cite> explores how formal verification can enhance user trust and interaction in everyday planning tasks through a human-in-the-loop interface, our work addresses a fundamentally different problem: the formal verification of LLM-based policies themselves within sequential decision-making environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce LLMs and then probabilistic model checking.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">token</em> <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is an atomic text element, such as a word, punctuation mark, or symbol.
A <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bag of tokens</em> <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite, nonempty set of tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(String)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A string <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite sequence of tokens chosen from some bag <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define the LLM function in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Large-Language-Model Function). ‣ 3.1 Large Language Models ‣ 3 Background ‣ Verifying Memoryless Sequential Decision-making of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Large-Language-Model Function)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An <em id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">LLM function</em> is a (in our case, deterministic)
total mapping</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\Sigma^{*}\;\longrightarrow\;\Sigma^{*}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\Sigma^{*}\;\longrightarrow\;\Sigma^{*},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that for every prompt <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\Sigma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\Sigma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> it returns a finite
<em id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">output string</em> <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}=f(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}=f(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An LLM generates text in an autoregressive manner (see Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Large Language Models ‣ 3 Background ‣ Verifying Memoryless Sequential Decision-making of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:195.1pt;height:110.2pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1.0,1.0) ;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="152.51" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 260.72 152.51" width="260.72"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,152.51) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(38.03,0) translate(0,72.89)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -37.47 -16.76 h 74.95 v 33.52 h -74.95 Z"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -32.86 -9.46)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 24.29)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 9.46)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.83em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="66.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">Input Text</span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 21.61)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 14.39 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.67em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="36.94"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">String</span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 140.76 29.53 L 73.09 29.53 C 70.03 29.53 67.56 27.05 67.56 23.99 L 67.56 -23.99 C 67.56 -27.05 70.03 -29.53 73.09 -29.53 L 140.76 -29.53 C 143.82 -29.53 146.3 -27.05 146.3 -23.99 L 146.3 23.99 C 146.3 27.05 143.82 29.53 140.76 29.53 Z M 67.56 -29.53"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 91.94 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.17em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="29.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">LLM</span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 180.99 -9.53)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 19.06)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 9.46)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 5 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="26.52"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">New</span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 19.07)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="38.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">Token</span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 212.85 57.83 C 212.85 65.35 206.76 71.44 199.25 71.44 C 191.73 71.44 185.64 65.35 185.64 57.83 C 185.64 50.32 191.73 44.23 199.25 44.23 C 206.76 44.23 212.85 50.32 212.85 57.83 Z M 199.25 57.83"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 193.87 54.37)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.08em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.07)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">+</span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 37.75 0 L 62.9 0"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 60.64 0)"><path d="M 5.9 0 L 0.89 1.87 L 2.4 0 L 0.89 -1.87 Z"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 146.57 0 L 171.72 0"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 169.46 0)"><path d="M 5.9 0 L 0.89 1.87 L 2.4 0 L 0.89 -1.87 Z"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 199.25 14.42 L 199.25 39.57"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 199.25 37.31)"><path d="M 5.9 0 L 0.89 1.87 L 2.4 0 L 0.89 -1.87 Z"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 185.76 61.15 C 119.75 79.34 65.09 65.11 19.44 20.11"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.71211 -0.70207 0.70207 -0.71211 21.05 21.7)"><path d="M 5.9 0 L 0.89 1.87 L 2.4 0 L 0.89 -1.87 Z"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -37.75 -30.81 M -37.73 -37.73 C -35.65 -41.88 -30.8 -44.63 -23.88 -44.62 L 78.52 -44.36 C 85.44 -44.34 90.29 -47.1 92.37 -51.25 C 94.44 -47.09 99.27 -44.31 106.19 -44.29 L 208.59 -44.04 C 215.51 -44.02 220.35 -41.24 222.41 -37.09"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 24.17 -65.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.82em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="135.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">Iterative Process Loop</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Autoregressive text generation process via an transformer. The transformer processes the input text, producing a probability distribution over the potential next tokens using its neural network. A token is stochastically selected from this distribution (in our setting, we force deterministic behavior by setting seeds) and added back into the text, which is then re-fed into the transformer, continuing the iterative text generation process.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a more detailed description of the underlying transformer architecture, we refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. (2017)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Probabilistic Model Checking</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">probability distribution</span> over a set <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a function <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\colon X\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\colon X\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{x\in X}\mu(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{x\in X}\mu(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of all distributions on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Distr(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Distr(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MDP)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <em id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">MDP</em> is a tuple <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(S,s_{0},Act,Tr,rew,AP,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(S,s_{0},Act,Tr,rew,AP,L)</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite, nonempty set of states; <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\in S</annotation></semantics></math> is an initial state; <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Act" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Act</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of actions; <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Tr\colon S\times Act\rightarrow Distr(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Tr\colon S\times Act\rightarrow Distr(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is a partial probability transition function;
<math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rew\colon S\times Act\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rew\colon S\times Act\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a reward function;
<math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AP</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of atomic propositions;
<math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\colon S\rightarrow 2^{AP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\colon S\rightarrow 2^{AP}</annotation></semantics></math> is a labeling function.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We employ a factored state representation where each state <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector of features <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},f_{2},...,f_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},f_{2},...,f_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> where each feature <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math> (state dimension).
The available actions in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Act(s)=\{a\in Act|P(s,a)\neq\bot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≠</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊥</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Act(s)=\{a\in Act|P(s,a)\neq\bot\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(s,a)\neq\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≠</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(s,a)\neq\bot</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\not\in Act(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\not\in Act(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <em id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">memoryless deterministic policy <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math></em> for an MDP <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a function <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon S\rightarrow Act" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon S\rightarrow Act</annotation></semantics></math> that maps a state <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> to action <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in Act" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in Act</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying a policy <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to an MDP <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> yields an <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">induced DTMC (Discrete-Time Markov Chains)</em> <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> where all non-determinism is resolved.
The interaction between the policy and environment is described in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Probabilistic Model Checking ‣ 3 Background ‣ Verifying Memoryless Sequential Decision-making of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:198.1pt;height:51.3pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1.0,1.0) ;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="71.01" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 264.93 71.01" width="264.93"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,71.01) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(126.86,0) translate(0,59.97)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFD9B3;" fill="#FFD9B3" stroke="#000000"><path d="M -59.06 -10.76 h 118.11 v 21.52 h -118.11 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -18.55 -3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.71em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="37.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">Policy</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFD9B3;" fill="#FFD9B3" stroke="#000000"><path d="M 19.69 -59.06 h 118.11 v 19.69 h -118.11 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 39.61 -53.94)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.71em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="79.03"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F2.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">Environment</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 59.33 0 L 78.74 0 L 78.74 -35.5"></path><g transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 78.74 -35.5)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 83.91 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="40.36"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F2.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">Action</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 19.41 -49.21 L 0 -49.21 L 0 -14.64"></path><g transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 0 -14.64)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -122.24 -52.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.49em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="117.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F2.pic1.4" class="ltx_text">New State, Reward</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">This diagram represents an RL system in which an agent interacts with an environment. The agent receives a state and a reward from the environment based on its previous action. The agent then uses this information to select the next action, which it sends to the environment.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Storm is a model checker.
It enables the verification of properties in induced DTMCs, with reachability properties being among the most fundamental.
These properties assess the probability of a system reaching a particular state.
For example, one might ask, “Is the probability of the system reaching an unsafe state less than 0.1?”
A property can be either <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">satisfied</em> or <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">violated</em>.
Detailed descriptions about specifying different PCTL properties can be found here <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hansson and Jonsson (1994)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baier and Katoen (2008)</a></cite>.
PCTL formulae are interpreted over the states of an induced DTMC.
In a slight abuse of notation, we use PCTL state formulas to denote probability values. That is, we sometimes write <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\bowtie p}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⋈</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\bowtie p}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> where we omit the threshold <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance,
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Fempty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Fempty)</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the reachability probability of eventually running out of fuel.
For this paper, we describe the PCTL properties used in the experiment section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">general workflow</em> for model checking with Storm is as follows (see Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Probabilistic Model Checking ‣ 3 Background ‣ Verifying Memoryless Sequential Decision-making of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):
First, the system is modeled using a language such as PRISM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">pri (????)</a></cite>.
Next, a (PCTL) property is formalized based on the system’s requirements.
Using these inputs, a model checker verifies whether the formalized property is satisfied or violated within the model and computes the exact probability that the property holds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hensel et al. (2022)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In probabilistic model checking, there is no universal “one-size-fits-all” solution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hensel et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
The most suitable tools and techniques depend significantly on the specific input model and properties being analyzed.
During model checking, model checkers can proceed “on the fly”, exploring only the parts of the DTMC most relevant to the verification.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:265.3pt;height:149.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1.0,1.0) ;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="206.34" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 357.8 206.34" width="357.8"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,206.34) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(126.38,0) translate(0,191.35)"><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 35.71 0 C 35.71 7.98 19.72 14.44 0 14.44 C -19.72 14.44 -35.71 7.98 -35.71 0 C -35.71 -7.98 -19.72 -14.44 0 -14.44 C 19.72 -14.44 35.71 -7.98 35.71 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -20.64 -2.91)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.98em;--ltx-fo-height:0.62em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.2" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="41.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">system</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 85.8 -50.45 C 85.8 -42.05 47.39 -35.23 0 -35.23 C -47.39 -35.23 -85.8 -42.05 -85.8 -50.45 C -85.8 -58.86 -47.39 -65.67 0 -65.67 C 47.39 -65.67 85.8 -58.86 85.8 -50.45 Z M 0 -50.45"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -56.06 -53.91)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.1em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="112.12"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">system description</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 32.34 -100.3 C 32.34 -92.66 17.86 -86.47 0 -86.47 C -17.86 -86.47 -32.34 -92.66 -32.34 -100.3 C -32.34 -107.94 -17.86 -114.14 0 -114.14 C 17.86 -114.14 32.34 -107.94 32.34 -100.3 Z M 0 -100.3"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -18.26 -105.11)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.61em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="36.13"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">model</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 215.88 -50.45 C 215.88 -42.19 188.33 -35.49 154.35 -35.49 C 120.36 -35.49 92.81 -42.19 92.81 -50.45 C 92.81 -58.72 120.36 -65.41 154.35 -65.41 C 188.33 -65.41 215.88 -58.72 215.88 -50.45 Z M 154.35 -50.45"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 115.45 -53.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.67em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.93" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.24)" width="78.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.4" class="ltx_text">requirements</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 203.65 -101.16 C 203.65 -92.9 181.57 -86.21 154.35 -86.21 C 127.12 -86.21 105.04 -92.9 105.04 -101.16 C 105.04 -109.43 127.12 -116.12 154.35 -116.12 C 181.57 -116.12 203.65 -109.43 203.65 -101.16 Z M 154.35 -101.16"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 124.1 -104.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.34em;--ltx-fo-height:0.67em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.93" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.24)" width="60.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.5" class="ltx_text">properties</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 45.2 -134.65 L -45.2 -134.65 C -48.26 -134.65 -50.74 -137.13 -50.74 -140.19 L -50.74 -156.79 C -50.74 -159.85 -48.26 -162.33 -45.2 -162.33 L 45.2 -162.33 C 48.26 -162.33 50.74 -159.85 50.74 -156.79 L 50.74 -140.19 C 50.74 -137.13 48.26 -134.65 45.2 -134.65 Z M -50.74 -162.33"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -46.12 -151.95)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.69em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="92.63"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.6" class="ltx_text">model checking</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -43.53 -176.96 C -43.53 -169.31 -61.95 -163.12 -84.68 -163.12 C -107.4 -163.12 -125.82 -169.31 -125.82 -176.96 C -125.82 -184.6 -107.4 -190.79 -84.68 -190.79 C -61.95 -190.79 -43.53 -184.6 -43.53 -176.96 Z M -84.68 -176.96"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -109.16 -181.76)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.54em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="48.97"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.7" class="ltx_text">satisfied</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 124.71 -176.96 C 124.71 -169.31 106.58 -163.12 84.21 -163.12 C 61.85 -163.12 43.72 -169.31 43.72 -176.96 C 43.72 -184.6 61.85 -190.79 84.21 -190.79 C 106.58 -190.79 124.71 -184.6 124.71 -176.96 Z M 84.21 -176.96"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 60.19 -181.76)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="48.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.8" class="ltx_text">violated</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -15 L 0 -31.08"></path><g transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -31.08)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 5.17 -28.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.94em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="54.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.9" class="ltx_text">modeling</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -66.23 L 0 -82.31"></path><g transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -82.31)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 5.17 -80.87)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.46em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="75.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.10" class="ltx_text">translates to</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 154.35 -65.97 L 154.35 -82.05"></path><g transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 154.35 -82.05)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 159.51 -79.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="67.3"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.11" class="ltx_text">formalizing</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -114.69 L 0 -130.78"></path><g transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -130.78)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 118.83 -112.05 L 49.47 -133.32"></path><g transform="matrix(-0.95605 -0.29317 0.29317 -0.95605 49.47 -133.32)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -41.97 -162.61 L -51.39 -165.77"></path><g transform="matrix(-0.94794 -0.31847 0.31847 -0.94794 -51.39 -165.77)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 41.75 -162.61 L 51.26 -165.82"></path><g transform="matrix(0.9474 -0.32002 0.32002 0.9474 51.26 -165.82)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Model checking workflow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hensel et al. (2022)</a></cite>. First, the system needs to be formally modeled, for instance, via PRISM. Then, the requirements are formalized, for instance, via PCTL. Eventually, both are inputted into the model checker, like Storm, which verifies the property.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodolodgy</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first introduce the notation and components required to represent LLM policies in memoryless sequential decision‐making tasks. We then describe our procedure for formally verifying these policies against safety properties.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Policy Representation via an LLM</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a sequential decision‐making task modeled as an MDP <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our goal is to implement a deterministic, memoryless policy <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon S\to\text{Act}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>Act</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon S\to\text{Act}</annotation></semantics></math> using an LLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S4.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">State Encoder <math id="S4.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\colon S\to\Sigma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\colon S\to\Sigma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a state <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(s)</annotation></semantics></math> produces a prompt string <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\Sigma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\Sigma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that encodes (i) the current state description, (ii) the dynamics or constraints of the task, and (iii) any additional context needed by the LLM.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM <math id="S4.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\Sigma^{*}\to\Sigma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\Sigma^{*}\to\Sigma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM takes the prompt <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=I(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=I(s)</annotation></semantics></math> and returns an output string <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}=f(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}=f(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. This string is intended to specify an action recommendation.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action Parser <math id="S4.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\colon\Sigma^{*}\to\text{Act}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Act</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\colon\Sigma^{*}\to\text{Act}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The parser <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> interprets the LLM’s output <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a valid action in <span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Act</span>, <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> returns that action; otherwise it resorts to a predefined default action or raises an error.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Combining these components yields a LLM policy</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(s)=O\bigl(f\bigl(I(s)\bigr)\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(s)=O\bigl(f\bigl(I(s)\bigr)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which we use to select actions at runtime.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>LLM Policy Verification</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an MDP <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of the sequential decision-making task, an LLM policy <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, an input encoder function <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, an action parser function <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, and a desired safety property (see Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 LLM Policy Verification ‣ 4 Methodolodgy ‣ Verifying Memoryless Sequential Decision-making of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), we incrementally build the induced DTMC of the policy <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and the MDP <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For every reachable state <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> via the LLM policy <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, we query for an action <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the underlying MDP <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, only states <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> reachable via that action <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in Act(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in Act(s)</annotation></semantics></math> are expanded.
The resulting DTMC <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> resolves all nondeterminism, with no open action choices (see Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 LLM Policy Verification ‣ 4 Methodolodgy ‣ Verifying Memoryless Sequential Decision-making of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), and is passed to the model checker Storm for verification, yielding the <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exact</em> results concerning satisfying the safety property or violating it.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
